.TH PEEKIN-SESSION 1 2026-09-25 "PeekIn 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
peekin-session \- walk-away lock and notices of failed face attempts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/libexec/peekin/peekin-session
.br
.B /usr/libexec/peekin/peekin-session \-\-dry\-run
.RB [ \-\-away
.IR seconds ]
.br
.B /usr/libexec/peekin/peekin-session \-\-check\-camera
.SH DESCRIPTION
The per-user helper of PeekIn.
It runs in every GNOME session as the systemd user service
.BR peekin-session.service ,
as the user, and does two things:
.TP
.B Notices of failed attempts
After the screen is unlocked, a desktop notification tells how many faces
were rejected (wrong face, or the head movement not followed) since the last
notice.
It reads the user's own history in
.IR /var/lib/peekin-activity .
.TP
.B Walk-away lock
Off by default.
When there has been no keyboard or mouse input for the away time, the camera
looks for a face every few seconds; with nobody in front of it for 10 seconds
the screen locks.
The camera is only used while there is no input, and is released as soon as
there is.
A busy camera (for example in a video call), a picture too dark to tell, and
a video that keeps the desktop from going idle never lead to a lock.
The helper only locks; unlocking always goes through
.BR pam_peekin (8)
or the password.
.PP
Both are set per user on the settings app's
.B Away lock
page, stored in
.IR ~/.config/peekin/session.ini :
.BR lock_when_away " (false), " away_secs " (60, 30 to 600), "
.BR notify_failed_attempts " (true) and"
.BR camera :
.B same
(the face login camera, the default),
.B colour
(the first colour camera),
.B infrared
(the first infrared camera, approved or not) or a device such as
.IR /dev/v4l/by-id/\&... .
The walk-away lock authenticates no one, so it may use a different camera than
face login, and ignores
.BR ir_only .
With no camera of the chosen kind connected it never locks.
The file is read again every few seconds.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print every decision (idle time, camera result) and never lock, with the
walk-away lock treated as on.
.TP
.BI \-\-away " seconds"
With
.BR \-\-dry\-run :
use this away time instead of the setting.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-camera
Look for a face once, with the camera chosen for the walk-away lock, and print
.BR Face ,
.B NoFace
or
.B Unknown
(too dark, or the camera unavailable; exit status 1).
.SH FILES
.I ~/.config/peekin/session.ini
.br
.I ~/.local/state/peekin/notified
\- the newest attempt already told about
.br
.I /var/lib/peekin-activity/
.SH NOTES
Stop it for one session with
.BR "systemctl \-\-user stop peekin-session" ,
or for good with
.BR "systemctl \-\-user disable peekin-session" .
Logs:
.BR "journalctl \-\-user \-u peekin-session" .
It uses GNOME's session bus interfaces (idle monitor, screen saver, session
manager, notifications).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peekin-settings (1),
.BR peekin-config (5),
.BR pam_peekin (8)
